# gitleaks baseline — 24 verified-benign historical findings.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# gitleaks-action picks its scope from the triggering event: on `push` and
# `pull_request` it scans only that event's commits. So every green run this
# repo has ever had was INCREMENTAL — a secret committed before the current
# push would never have been caught. The first full-history scan (2026-08-08,
# via the new workflow_dispatch trigger) reported these 24 findings.
#
# Each was opened at its flagged commit and verified to be a placeholder, a
# test fixture, or documentation — NOT a real credential. Nothing was rotated
# because there was nothing to rotate. Evidence, by group, below.
#
# THIS IS A BASELINE, NOT A SUPPRESSION RULE.
# Entries are exact `commit:path:rule:line` fingerprints. They pin these 24
# specific historical findings and nothing else: a real secret committed to any
# of these same files — including the spec files — still fails the scan. Never
# replace these with a path glob (e.g. `*.spec.ts`); that would blind the
# scanner to exactly the case it exists to catch.
#
# Re-verify with:  gitleaks detect --redact -v

